    "shop_smart":  ["\u003cstrong\u003eGreek yogurt in the big tub is the value buy.\u003c/strong\u003e Single-serve cups cost several times as much per ounce, and this dish uses a real amount of it.","\u003cstrong\u003eCanned crushed tomatoes in the large cans are the cheapest tomato per ounce.\u003c/strong\u003e This sauce uses a lot, so buying the big cans instead of the small ones matters here.","\u003cstrong\u003ePaprika butter is two ingredients and it makes the dish.\u003c/strong\u003e Butter and paprika, warmed together until the color bleeds out. That is the entire fancy finishing sauce.","\u003cstrong\u003eFresh mint is the priciest small item on this list.\u003c/strong\u003e Dried mint is actually traditional in Turkish cooking and costs a fraction, so it is a fair swap if the fresh price is ugly that week.","\u003cstrong\u003eThe penne is protein penne.\u003c/strong\u003e It comes in a 14.5 ounce box, not a pound, so count boxes off 14.5. It costs more than ordinary pasta, and between that and the yogurt this dish carries its protein honestly."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Boil the penne in well salted water to al dente, drain, and toss it with a little of the olive oil so it does not stick.",
                    "Heat the rest of the olive oil in a large pot and cook the diced yellow onions until softened.",
                    "Add most of the garlic, then the ground beef, and cook until browned with no pink remaining.",
                    "Stir in the tomato paste, ground cumin, paprika, dried oregano, red pepper flakes, salt, and black pepper and cook until the paste darkens.",
                    "Add the crushed tomatoes and simmer uncovered until the sauce is thick and no longer watery.",
                    "Stir the remaining garlic and the lemon juice into the greek yogurt with a pinch of salt, and keep it cold.",
                    "Melt the butter with a spoonful of paprika until it foams and turns red, then chop the fresh mint for the finish."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "Weigh the pasta, the beef sauce, and the yogurt separately, subtracting the empty-pot weight from Step 1 where it applies, and divide each by 14. Pasta down first, sauce over it, and store the garlic yogurt and paprika butter in small separate containers so the yogurt stays cold and does not break. Every serving comes in at \u003cstrong\u003e{{cal}} calories with {{protein}} grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e. Assemble each one right before you eat it.",
